Since the parallelepipedal solid {KA|sKA} is similar to {LC|sLC}, therefore {KA|sKA} has to {LC|sLC} the ratio triplicate of that which {AB} has to {CD}. For the same reason {ME|sME} has to {NG|sNG} the ratio triplicate of that which {EF} has to {GH}. [!just XI.33]

And {AB} is to {CD} as {EF} is to {GH}. Therefore {AK|sKA} is to {LC|sLC} as {ME|sME} is to {NG|sNG}.

<hr width="50%">

Next as the solid {AK|sKA} is to the solid {LC|sLC}, so let the solid {ME|sME} be to the solid {NG|sNG}.

I say that the straight line {AB} is to {CD} as {EF} is to {GH}. Since, again, {KA|sKA} has to {LC|sLC} the ratio triplicate of that which {AB} has to {CD}, and {ME|sME} also has to {NG|sNG} the ratio triplicate of that which {EF} has to {GH}, and {KA|sKA} is to {LC|sLC} as {ME|sME} is to {NG|sNG}, therefore {AB} is to {CD} as {EF} is to {GH}. [!just XI.33]

Therefore, *if four straight lines are proportional, then parallelepipedal solids on them which are similar and similarly described are also proportional; and, if the parallelepipedal solids on them which are similar and similarly described are proportional, then the straight lines themselves are also proportional.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

This proposition completes the theory of volumes of parallelepipeds.

In the proof of this proposition it is assumed that two ratios are equal if and only if their triplicate ratios are equal. The required proof is long and detailed, but not difficult.
